# break, continue and else

- break exits the loop immediately; the code after the loop runs next.
- continue skips the rest of the current iteration and jumps to the next one.

```python
for n in range(10):
    if n == 5:
        break
    if n % 2 == 0:
        continue
    print(n)   # 1, then 3
```

Loops can have an else block too. It runs when the loop finishes normally, and it is skipped if the loop ends with break. It is handy for searches:

```python
for n in [3, 7, 9]:
    if n % 2 == 0:
        print("found an even number")
        break
else:
    print("no even numbers")   # runs: no break happened
```
